  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sudan Crisis Response: Multi-Sector Emergency Response to Ethiopian Migrants, Refugees and Third Country Nationals in Ethiopia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The conflict that broke out in Sudan on 15 April 2023 between the Rapid Support Forces and Sudanese Amred Force continues to have devastating consequences for millions of civilians, with 24.7 million people estimated to be in need of humanitarian assistance by December 2023. A severe humanitarian crisis has also unfolded in neighbouring countries, which to date are hosting over 1 million people who fled Sudan in search of safety and access to goods and basic essential services. This includes Ethiopia, where over 79,300 people have arrived as of September 2023. 
 
Through this allocation, the International Organization for Migration (IOM), United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees, and World Health Organization are supporting government-led efforts to address the protection and urgent needs of refugees, returning Ethiopian migrants and third country nationals fleeing the conflict in Sudan in the affected regions in Ethiopia. The project ensures that vulnerable arrivals are not left stranded in remote border areas and are able to receive immediate life-saving assistance and return home or relocate to designated camps. IOM&#x2019;s multi-sector emergency assistance will benefit 32,000 individuals including with transportation assistance to safety, protection assistance at POEs, emergency water, sanitation and hygiene, multi-purpose cash, and health assistance.</narrative>
  </description>
